title: Shadows & Substance – Old Testament Practices and Salvation by Faith
description: How the Tabernacle, the sacrifices and the Law were shadows that pointed to Christ, while righteousness before God has always come by faith.
output: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-3-ot-shadows-and-substance.html

# Doctrine Week 3. One deck from two old pages: the seven-slide main deck and the longer follow-up page
# ("The Purpose of Old Testament Practices & Salvation by Faith"), folded together in the main deck's order.

=== Introduction
--- Shadows & Substance [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School · Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County
heading: Shadows & Substance
sub: Old Testament Practices and Salvation by Faith
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- The question
lead: There is a misunderstanding among many about the Old Testament’s commands for building a temple to offer blood sacrifice to God. People ask, how does this line up with the understanding that we are saved by faith in the sacrifice of Jesus alone in the New Testament? Does this mean works were needed to be saved in the Old Testament?
p: God commanded Moses to build the Tabernacle.
big: Exodus 25:8
p: God also commanded Moses that the people would bring sacrifices and offerings, culminating each year in the Day of Atonement, a statute for Israel to keep continually.
scripture: Leviticus 16:34

--- A shadow on the ground
lead: Imagine a shadow on the ground. It shows the **shape** of a person but is not the person. The Tabernacle and its sacrifices were shadows cast by Jesus, the real thing standing just out of sight.
refs: Hebrews 8:1-5; Hebrews 10:1
callout: **Key Point:** The Tabernacle, Temple, sacrifices, washings, and dietary laws were God’s instructions to Israel, but they never saved a single soul. Salvation has always been by faith, exactly as it is for us today in Christ.

=== I. Old Testament practices: sanctifying the flesh
--- The Tabernacle & Temple
lead: A dwelling place, first mobile, then permanent, where God’s presence would meet with His people.
panel: The Tabernacle | “Let them make me a sanctuary; that I may dwell among them.” [[Exodus 25:8]]
panel: The Temple | Later made permanent: the Ark, the mercy seat, the glory-cloud filling the house of the {Lord}. [[1 Kings 8:8-10]] [[1 Kings 8:10-11]]
callout: These were special places marking Israel as God’s own nation, but they did not save souls. The ritual restored **ceremonial cleanliness**, allowing a person to approach God’s presence without defiling the sanctuary. It was never about justifying the soul, which required faith. [[Hebrews 9:1-10]]

--- Sacrifices, washings & dietary laws
panel: Sacrifices | Animal offerings, like on the Day of Atonement, covered sin temporarily. Blood cleaned the outside; it could not fix the heart forever. Their yearly repetition underscores a *temporary, external* effect. Ritual purity to maintain access to the tabernacle, not a transformation of the heart. [[Leviticus 16:15-19]]
panel: Washings & dietary laws | Ceremonial cleansings and food rules kept Israel distinct and addressed ritual uncleanness. These were about physical purity and obedience, not eternal salvation. [[Leviticus 15:13-15]] [[Numbers 19:1-2]] [[Numbers 19:17-19]]
panel-warn: Carnal ordinances | “Which stood only in meats and drinks, and divers washings, and carnal ordinances, imposed on them until the time of reformation.” These were “carnal” (fleshly) rules, temporary until Christ came. [[Hebrews 9:10]]

--- Purifying the flesh, not the soul
scripture: Hebrews 9:13
p: These rituals purified the body, not the soul.

=== II. Purpose & limitation
--- Why God required them
lead: To make Israel holy, set apart among pagan nations, and to point ahead to a greater sacrifice: Jesus, who would truly save. These rituals were a “picture” of the spiritual cleansing and atonement Christ would accomplish. [[Hebrews 9:11-15]]
callout: These rituals were like a picture book pointing to Jesus, the real Savior.

--- Where they fell short
lead: **Limitation:** They could never permanently remove sin. Sacrifices offered “year by year continually” could never make the worshipper perfect.
scripture: Hebrews 10:1-4
p: Animal sacrifices were a temporary fix, like a bandage, not a cure.

--- The Day of Atonement
panel: The slain goat | A sin offering whose blood was shed for the sins of the nation, a picture of Christ’s atoning blood. [[Leviticus 16:15-19]]
panel: The High Priest | Carried the blood of the slain goat into the Holy of Holies within the Tabernacle, sprinkling it upon the Mercy Seat, foreshadowing Christ our great High Priest. [[Hebrews 9:1-10]]
panel: The scapegoat | Bore Israel’s sins into the wilderness, symbolizing guilt removed. [[Leviticus 16:20-22]]
take: Jesus is the scapegoat, the Lamb slain, and the High Priest.

--- More shadows of Christ
fig: The Passover lamb | Exodus 12:13 | The temporary, external protection of the Passover lamb prefigures Christ’s blood, which provides eternal spiritual redemption.
fig: The scapegoat | Leviticus 16:20-22 | A ritual act pointing to Christ, who “bare our sins in his own body” [[1 Peter 2:24]], but symbolic and external, requiring annual repetition, unlike Christ’s once-for-all sacrifice.

=== III. Salvation by faith
--- Abraham believed God
lead: Before any Tabernacle or Law existed, Abraham was already saved by faith. Before the Law, salvation was by faith. The sacrificial system dealt with ceremonial cleanliness, but righteousness before God was always tied to faith, not ritual.
big: Genesis 15:6
p: Abraham was saved by believing God’s promise, not by sacrifices or rules, and long before circumcision was given at 99. [[Romans 4:1-5]] [[Romans 4:9-13]]

--- Faith in the Old Testament
lead: The Old Testament itself critiques leaning on sacrifice for spiritual standing. God sought faith and obedience over the blood of bulls and goats.
lit: Counted for righteousness | Genesis 15:6
lit: The just shall live by faith | Habakkuk 2:4
fig: A broken and contrite heart | Psalm 51:16-17
fig: Cease to do evil | Isaiah 1:11; Isaiah 1:16-17
refs: Hebrews 10:4-6; Hosea 6:6

--- Christ fulfills the law
lead: Jesus’ death was the one-time, perfect sacrifice that saves the soul forever, accomplishing both sanctification and forgiveness, resolving what the Old Testament system never could. [[Hebrews 10:5-14]]
panel: Wisdom, righteousness, sanctification, redemption | Jesus doesn’t just cover sin. He makes us holy in God’s eyes. [[1 Corinthians 1:30]]
panel: I will remember their sins no more | The covenant that makes it so. [[Hebrews 10:15-17]]

--- Saved by faith today
big: Romans 5:1
p: We are saved by trusting Christ, not by rituals or works. Faith gives peace with God that no sacrifice could fully give.
take: Old Testament rituals were like a map leading to Jesus, the destination.

=== IV. The new heaven and new earth
--- No temple needed
lead: In the New Jerusalem, no physical Temple is needed. God and the Lamb are the temple. We do not need rituals or buildings, because Jesus’ sacrifice makes us holy forever.
scripture: Revelation 21:22
refs: Revelation 21:21-24
p: Christ’s work means we are fully sanctified by faith, not by repeated washings or sacrifices. The Old Testament pointed to this future, where God’s presence is enough.

--- We are His temple
refs: 1 Corinthians 6:19-20; 2 Corinthians 6:16; 1 Peter 2:5
take: The Tabernacle was like a tent for God’s presence; now, Jesus Himself is our home.

=== V. Application
--- Key takeaways
panel: Fleshly sanctification | Sacrifices and washings restored ritual purity to allow access to the tabernacle. They addressed external states but could not cleanse the conscience or remove sin’s guilt.
panel: Spiritual sanctification | Righteousness before God came through faith. Sacrifices were never a substitute for faith, but a means to maintain covenantal relationship in the physical sphere.
panel: Christ as fulfillment | Christ’s sacrifice transcends the Old Testament system, purifying both flesh and spirit. His blood cleanses the conscience and grants eternal redemption, what the rituals could only symbolize.
callout: **Practical application:** Encourage trust in Christ’s finished work, not in personal efforts to earn salvation. Read [[Hebrews 9:1-28|Hebrews 9]] and [[Hebrews 10:1-39|Hebrews 10]] at home to see how Jesus fulfills the Law.

=== VI. Conclusion
--- Conclusion
lead: The Tabernacle, Temple, sacrifices, washings, and dietary laws were God’s way to teach Israel about holiness and point to Jesus. Salvation has always been by faith, and Christ’s sacrifice makes us holy forever, as we will see in Heaven, with no need for a Temple.
- Old Testament practices addressed **outward** holiness, not the saving of the soul.
- Salvation is by faith alone, as it was for Abraham, and is for us in Christ.
- Jesus’ sacrifice makes us holy forever, replacing every ritual. [[2 Corinthians 5:21]]
big: 1 Corinthians 1:30
take: No sacrifice but Jesus. You are saved by faith, and Jesus has already made you holy in God’s eyes. Rest in that!
